To isolate external conditions from issues specific to your business, consider conducting a SWOT analysis (Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, and Threats). This will help you identify internal factors that are within your control and external factors that are outside of your control. By separating these factors, you can better understand whether the issues you're facing are due to your strategy or the overall market conditions.

When applying the DCF method in a fluctuating market, you can account for uncertainty by using sensitivity analysis or scenario planning. This involves creating multiple scenarios with different assumptions about future cash flows, such as a base case, a best-case scenario, and a worst-case scenario. By analyzing these different scenarios, you can get a sense of the range of possible outcomes and adjust your valuation accordingly.

Additionally, you can use probability-weighted cash flows to account for uncertainty. This involves assigning probabilities to different possible outcomes and then using these probabilities to weight the cash flows. For example, if you think there's a 50% chance of a high-growth scenario and a 50% chance of a low-growth scenario, you can weight the cash flows accordingly.

It's also important to consider using a risk premium when discounting cash flows to account for the uncertainty in the market. This will help you to adjust the valuation to reflect the increased risk associated with the business.

By using these methods, you can increase the objectivity of your valuation and get a better sense of the true value of your business, even in uncertain market conditions.

